.TH QARGI 1 "2026-11-09" "qargi 0.2.8"
.SH NAME
qargi \- A gathering place for agent work: dispatch, review, remember
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qargi
.I command
[\fIverb\fR] [\fIarguments\fR]
.br
.B qargi help
[\fIcommand\fR|\fItopic\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
qargi dispatches one\-off, task\-scoped development tasks to sandboxed agents and takes the work back as reviewable patches. An agent is a member on this machine (local, the default) or a cloud agent (only when you ask for it in that request). Agents work in their own clone and never push. A team adds a lead that coordinates members for build, write and review on one task. Everything qargi stores about you and your projects lives outside every repository. Inside a project's repository you can leave out its name.
.PP
Every command takes \fB\-\-help\fR. A command that changes shared resources prints a plan and applies it only with \fB\-\-plan\fR and \fB\-\-approved\fR; a cloud command needs \fB\-\-asked\fR. See APPROVALS.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
print the result as one JSON object with the verb, the plan, the token, the command that applies it and the exit status (the output instead, when the verb printed no plan token); never supplies \-\-plan or \-\-approved.
.SH CONCEPTS
Task: you describe one task and an executable check that decides it is done. An agent works on it in its own clone and hands back numbered patches, one per commit, with a manifest. Nothing is pushed.
.PP
Roles are build, write, review, test and lead: build changes code, write writes the operational docs, review judges a change and changes no file, test writes and runs tests, lead coordinates a team. Each role is defined by its role text, which comes with the task block. The local workers are members you name and give roles (qargi agent add NAME \-\-roles build,test); qargi agent for ROLE prints the member that serves it. A role has its own model, effort, skills and packages, stored in the project's answers.
.PP
Local or cloud: local (a member on this machine) is the default. The cloud is used only when your request for that task names it, and every cloud verb records your words with \-\-asked.
.PP
You decide: the coordinator re\-verifies everything an agent returns (qargi review check), applies it as separate commits, and asks you before anything is pushed.
.SH APPROVALS
A command that changes something shared prints a plan first. The plan ends with a plan token, a hash of what would be done.
.PP
To apply, run the same command with \-\-plan TOKEN \-\-approved "your words". If the plan changed in between, the token no longer matches and nothing happens.
.PP
Add \-\-json to any verb to get the plan as one JSON object instead of text: {verb, plan, token, apply, exit}, where apply is the command line to run once the person has agreed (the call as given, without \-\-json, plus \-\-plan TOKEN and \-\-approved with a placeholder for the person's own words). A verb that printed no plan token (it ran, or refused) gives {verb, plan: null, token: null, output, exit}; the exit status is the verb's and its error messages stay on stderr. \-\-json never supplies \-\-plan or \-\-approved, and a call that carries them runs as it would without \-\-json, wrapped the same way with a null token. A verb that describes a \-\-json option of its own keeps it.
.PP
\-\-asked "your request" is required by every cloud verb: it records that you asked for the cloud. Both are written to an append\-only audit log in the state directory.
.PP
qargi lead grant and revoke are interactive instead: you confirm each step at the terminal, and that is the approval.
.PP
The rules. The token covers the plan the person saw, and an apply refuses when the plan is no longer that one. The \-\-approved text is recorded, not verified: it is not proof that a person agreed. Never allow\-list a gated verb in the runtime's settings: the permission prompt is the person's real control. A member never applies: it reports the plan and its token, and the lead shows them to the person (what the member's guard refuses: qargi help guard).
.SH GUARD
The guard hooks a member's calls: a barrier, not a wall; qargi local verify is its backstop.
.PP
It refuses verbs that change shared state: skill and tool new, import, seal, remove, unlink, link, enable, disable, accept and unaccept; discover install; import; packs apply, seed, link and unlink; project set (models, release, test_command, gates, env, site, backlog, packs, runs, upstream, git, review, roles.ROLE.tools, a whole role), init, new, link and unlink; provider new, import, seal, remove, enable, disable, attest, accept, check and adapter\-run (the rest read); release publish, sign, site, assets and fix; marshal claim, set, renew, release, take, takeover, finish, enable, disable, key, signers and resign (handoff only with \-\-write); feature start, finish, verify and park; branch prune; changelog renumber without \-\-check; upstream status, sync; backlog done; ref record; memory init, move, split, merge and copy; cloud set, unset and login; upgrade; local run, prepare, verify and clean; agent remove; review apply; team run; env plan and apply; task.
.PP
It refuses writes in the pack library, credentials, the install record, the runtime's skills, agents and plugin directories, the installed copy, the state's render, providers, marshal, feature and saved\-run directories, its ref journal, the memory tiers, ~/.config/anthropic, and the lead's git configuration and session files. Reading stays allowed except the lead's signing key: copying, linking, archiving or searching its directory is refused. It refuses git push, git remote changes, git \-c or config of keys that run programs (alias.*), gh, ssh, scp, sftp and rsync to a host.
.PP
A command is cut at every ; && || | and newline, inside quotes and heredocs too, and a substitution is a command: no refused verb, push or tag in text, unless every command is echo, cat, tee, grep or git commit (then text is data, as is a heredoc body cat or tee gets, bar lines starting qargi). Commit with git commit \-F FILE. Piped or here\-string shell programs are read; curl | sh and bash < f are refused. A cd it cannot read refuses relative writes.
.PP
The read\-only class (review) writes only OUT and TMP: every change to the clone is refused, and a write through a name the guard cannot read. Nobody copies, extracts or links into the run directory.
.PP
Setting QARGI_NESTED, which skips a plan token check, or setting or unsetting QARGI_LEAD, is refused.
.PP
A refusal is a result: report it; do not work around it.
.SH NAMING
Project and role are single lower\-case tokens. An instance is <project>\-<role>, for example acme\-build. A session is <instance>\-<task\-slug>, and the agent's branch is agent/<task\-slug>.
.PP
Cloud resources follow <project>\-<component>[\-<role>]: the agent <project>\-<role> (acme\-build), the deployment <project>\-deployment\-<role>, the shared <project>\-environment, and the stores <project>\-memory and <project>\-reference. The lead is <project>\-lead, and so is its agent.
.PP
qargi instances normalize renames existing resources to match.
.SH MEMORY
<project>\-memory is where agents write verified how\-to facts they learn, under their own role folder (shared/ is curated and reached by promotion). <project>\-reference is read\-only: rules, workflow and conventions you and the coordinator curate. Both have shared/ for every role and roles/<role>/ for one.
.PP
An agent reads each store's README.md, then shared/INDEX.md and its role's index, then only the notes its task needs. The canon (qargi canon) fixes the layout and the note format. Read one note with qargi memory get.
.PP
qargi memory split, merge and copy move notes between two stores (directories, .jsonl dumps or store ids). Nothing is deleted: what leaves a store is archived under /_archive/<date>/, a collision goes to /_conflicts/<date>/, and the origin of every note written is added to its Sources: line.
.SH PUSH
Only the lead ever pushes, never a member, and only as the project's lead.push grant says. The default is nothing.
.PP
This Claude Code session as lead: lead.push.local lets it push agent/* branches and open pull requests without asking each time.
.PP
A cloud lead, or one started in the console: lead.push.cloud = branches. It has a GitHub tool with a fixed allowlist and the merge tool asked of you. Set it up with qargi lead grant. A merge or a release always needs your explicit approval.
.PP
The GitHub tool cannot create releases; those stay with this session.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Set up
.TP
.B qargi project
Store the answers about a project, once, outside the repository.
.PP
The answers are the repository, its long\-lived branches, the roles that exist (build, write, review, test) with each role's base branch, model, effort, packages and skills, the memory stores, the budget, and optionally a lead with teams and a push grant. Provisioning and dispatch read them. Push policy per role is none: an agent never pushes; the lead's grant is separate (qargi help push).
.RS
.TP
.BI "template " "NAME"
Print an answers file with defaults.
.br
Edit it, then store it with init. models.provider is empty and must be set (the provider the project's agents run on; there is no default); project validate reports it while it is not. No role names a model: it takes the provider's tier once one is set.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "init " "NAME"
Validate and store an answers file.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-answers " "FILE"
the answers file to store.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-force
replace stored answers.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "new " "NAME"
Start a new project: its directory, a git repository on the methodology's branches and generic files.
.br
Prints the plan (directory, branches, files, deployment options with instructions) and a token; with \-\-plan and \-\-approved it creates the directory, runs git init, writes the generic files in a first commit and creates the long\-lived branches. Nothing is pushed and no remote is created: say where it goes with git. The files mention no tool of yours. They include an AGENTS.md with a Development environment section and a Makefile with stub bootstrap and doctor targets (the doctor fails until you fill it in); see qargi help environment.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-methodology " "NAME"
the methodology (default gitflow).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-dir " "DIR"
where to create it (default ./NAME; must be absent or empty).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-param " "NAME=VALUE"
a methodology parameter as name=value (repeat), for example test_command; gates=JSON declares gates, which are stored in the answers too when \-\-url is given.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-url " "URL"
the repository URL; with it the qargi answers are stored too.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-deployment " "ID"
a deployment option to record and explain (see qargi methodology deployment).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-provider " "NAME"
the provider the project's agents run on (models.provider, with its models); not stored when omitted (qargi provider list).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-provider\-local " "NAME"
the runtime of the local surface (models.provider.local); use it with \-\-provider\-cloud instead of \-\-provider.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-provider\-cloud " "NAME"
the runtime of the cloud surface (models.provider.cloud).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "attach " "[DIR]"
Attach to an existing repository: read it, show the answers and the gaps, store on approval.
.br
Reads the repository (branches, tags, version and changelog files, test command, CI) and changes nothing in it. Prints the answers it would store, the gaps against the methodology and what it could not determine, and a plan token. With \-\-plan and \-\-approved it stores the answers outside the repository. The safe gaps are closed by qargi project adopt, the rest by hand. It reads the tree of the methodology's integration branch (develop), else of the default branch, else of HEAD, and says which; never the working tree. It prints origin as read and the https url it normalises to. When the stored answers declare gates it lists them after the test command line (tier, name, command, the feature tier first) and says they replace the detected test command. If answers are already stored it shows stored against detected, lists the fields the stored file lacks, stays read\-only and gives no plan token; with \-\-force the plan replaces what it detects and keeps what it cannot (the roles' models and effort, cloud, memory, budget). It reports what it found of the development environment (section, task runner, bootstrap and doctor commands) and lists the pieces that are missing as gaps. It never guesses a provider: models.provider (and the roles' models) are stored only with \-\-provider NAME, and without it the output says so; with \-\-force \-\-provider fills in a provider that is not set and never replaces one.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-name " "NAME"
the project name (default: from the remote or the directory).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-methodology " "NAME"
the methodology to compare with (default gitflow).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-url " "URL"
the repository URL, when it has no origin remote.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-provider " "NAME"
the provider the project's agents run on (models.provider, with its models); not stored when omitted (qargi provider list).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-provider\-local " "NAME"
the runtime of the local surface (models.provider.local); use it with \-\-provider\-cloud instead of \-\-provider.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-provider\-cloud " "NAME"
the runtime of the cloud surface (models.provider.cloud).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-force
replace the detected answers, keep the rest.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "adopt " "[NAME]"
Adopt an attached repository: create the missing long\-lived branches and the missing methodology files on a branch to review.
.br
Prints the plan and a token: the local branches it would create (at the default branch tip; main stands for master; none is moved) and the files from the methodology that do not exist, written as one commit on agent/adopt\-NAME cut from develop. With \-\-plan and \-\-approved it creates exactly that. Existing branches and files are never changed, nothing is pushed or checked out, and a second run finds nothing to do. Changelog format, tag format and CI content are listed as left for you, and so are the bootstrap and doctor targets a repository with a task runner lacks (an existing file is not edited), and long\-lived branches that share no history (a master made as a snapshot has no merge base with develop): none is joined here, and the line says that release push joins the history of master. The AGENTS.md section it drafts names the repository's own runner. Refused (exit 2) with a dirty working tree, a detached HEAD, no commits, a project that is not attached, and in a member run.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-methodology " "NAME"
the methodology (default: the project's, else gitflow).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "set " "[NAME] PATH VALUE"
Change one answer.
.br
PATH is dotted (budget_cents, roles.write.effort, lead.push); VALUE is JSON or a plain string. models.extensions and models.accept_no_barrier are refused here: they change only through qargi provider enable|disable and qargi provider accept. roles.ROLE.tools is the list of the tools a role needs beyond its apt packages, each {kind, name, ...}: kind go (name a package path, version a tag: go install NAME@VERSION), kind binary (name the file name, version, url of the file itself, sha256 of it: downloaded to ~/.local/bin and kept only when the hash matches) and kind submodule (name as in .gitmodules, path: checked out in each member clone by qargi local prepare). env plan lists them, env apply installs them and env check reports one that is missing or wrong; a member's guard refuses setting them (and a whole role), as it refuses release.*, test_command, gates, env.*, site.* and backlog.*. backlog.file is the markdown file of the project's backlog (qargi backlog); a path outside the repository is fine. git.author ('Name <email>') is the author qargi review apply gives the commits it applies, and review.keep_author true keeps the member's author instead; review.scrub (a command line or a list of words) is run by qargi review check (and by qargi review apply, unless a passing check recorded the same series) on the applied series with the range in QARGI_SCRUB_BASE and QARGI_SCRUB_TIP, and review.trailer_policy reject fails a commit with a Co\-Authored\-By or Generated\-with trailer (none is the default); a member's guard refuses setting them. runs.owned_refs is a list of glob patterns for the refs a runtime or harness creates in the repository, which local verify and clean accept and name (see local verify); the guard refuses it to a member too.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "show " "[NAME]"
Print the stored answers and what they mean.
.br
The answers go to standard output as JSON (so they can be piped). When the project declares gates, which replace the test command in feature verify and release verify, they are listed on standard error (tier, name, command, the feature tier first).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B list
List the stored projects.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "validate " "FILE"
Check an answers file without storing it (a WARN line for each pack linked to a role that is not in the pack library, and for a profile that still carries the shipped template header).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "profile " "SUBCOMMAND [NAME]"
Check the repository's profile: the shipped template header, and gate commands the repository does not know (check).
.br
Read only, so a member may run it. check reads the profile (the gates, rules and review steps of a repository, outside the repository) and prints FAIL for each line at the top that is still the shipped example's (the Example profile title and the Copy to line of examples/myproject/profile.md), exit 1; and WARN for each backticked command in its Gates section that is found in none of the repository's instruction files and Makefile targets (a make target of the Makefile, the command's text in one of the three files, or a script that exists), exit 0. No profile is fine (said, exit 0); a \-\-profile file that is not there is exit 2. Without NAME the project is the one in effect, else the repository of the current directory. project validate prints the header finding as a WARN line.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-profile " "FILE"
the profile file to read (default: ~/.qargi/config/profiles/<repository>.md, the repository being the project's directory name).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "link " "[PROJECT]"
Make this work tree load the project's skills, agents and memory from ~/.qargi.
.br
One plan and one token for every resource, each placed and linked the way the project's runtime says (a directory link, one link per entry, or a copy). A real directory in the way is moved to NAME.qargi\-backup, never deleted. Run it inside a work tree to link that one; nothing records which work trees exist. A member's guard refuses it.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-resource " "LIST"
what to link: skills, agents, memory (comma separated; default every one the runtime links and that exists).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "unlink " "[PROJECT]"
Undo project link in this work tree and restore the backups.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-resource " "LIST"
what to link: skills, agents, memory (comma separated; default every one the runtime links and that exists).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B qargi methodology
List and inspect the development and release methodologies.
.PP
A methodology is the way a project is developed and released, as data: its branches, versioning and changelog, test tiers, CI, release steps and deployment options, and the generic files a new project starts with. One ships with qargi (gitflow); add your own under ~/.qargi/config/methodologies/NAME/ (methodology.json and a scaffold/ directory). These verbs only read; qargi project new and adopt write, and only after a plan.
.RS
.TP
.B list
List the methodologies and where they live.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "show " "NAME"
Print a methodology: branches, tests, CI, release steps, parameters, scaffold, deployment options.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "check " "[NAME]"
Validate one methodology, or all of them.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "files " "NAME"
Print the scaffold rendered with the parameters, writing nothing.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-param " "NAME=VALUE"
a parameter as name=value (repeat).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "deployment " "NAME [ID]"
List the deployment options, or print one option's instructions.
.RE
.TP
.B qargi cloud
Which ant profile and workspace each project uses.
.PP
A project can be bound to an ant profile and the workspace that profile must reach (the project's optional cloud answer). Every qargi command about that project then runs ant under that profile, and a cloud call is refused if the profile no longer reaches the workspace, so work never lands in another project's workspace. A project without a binding uses ant's active profile. qargi stores and checks the binding; it never reads or writes a token. login runs ant auth login on your terminal; a member or a script cannot.
.RS
.TP
.B list
Print every project's binding.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "show " "[PROJECT]"
Print one project's binding.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "set " "[PROJECT]"
Bind a project to an ant profile and a workspace.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-profile " "NAME"
the ant profile name.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-workspace\-id " "ID"
the workspace the profile must reach (wrkspc_...).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "unset " "[PROJECT]"
Remove a project's binding.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "check " "[PROJECT]"
Check that the profile exists and reaches the workspace (reads the ant profile only).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "login " "[PROJECT]"
Log the project's profile in, bound to its workspace (your terminal only).
.RE
.TP
.B qargi agent
Name the members: the local agents that do the work.
.PP
A member is a local agent you name, rendered from the one member definition qargi ships, with the roles it serves: build, write, review, test (all four by default). The render is kept in the configuration directory (agents/PROJECT/NAME.EXT beside agents.json, outside every repository) and written from there to the runtime's agents location; a checkout cleaned or cloned afresh gets it back with agent refresh. The task block names the role and carries its role text (roles/<role>/ROLE.md); there is no skill to load. Review is read\-only as a rule: the guard refuses changes to the clone under that role. Under every role it refuses the verbs that change a skill (qargi skill|tool new, import, seal, remove, link, unlink, enable, disable; qargi discover install; qargi packs apply) and writes in the pack library, the credentials directory and install.json, ~/.claude/skills and any .claude/skills directory; it fails closed. Dispatch spawns the member qargi agent for ROLE prints.
.RS
.TP
.BI "add " "NAME"
Create a member under a name you choose.
.br
A member belongs to a project, and the definition is rendered by the provider of that project. Refuses a name that exists, a name that is not kebab\-case, the name of a built\-in agent type of that provider, no \-\-project (exit 2), and a project with no provider (exit 2: there is no default provider). Nothing is written to a runtime's user\-level directory.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-roles " "LIST"
comma\-separated roles it serves (default build,write,review,test).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-model " "MODEL"
model alias or id for the definition (default: the project's model for the first role, else the shipped default; see agent models).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-effort " "LEVEL"
effort level, written into the definition and recorded with the member (default as for \-\-model).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project the member belongs to (required): its models.provider renders it, the render is kept in the configuration directory (agents/PROJECT/) and the runtime's file goes where the runtime links project agents (the checkout, hidden from git, or the project render once `project link` is applied).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B list
List the members of every project, their roles and definitions.
.br
One row per member, with no header line: PROJECT, NAME, ROLES (comma separated), MODEL/EFFORT, FILE, then in brackets MISSING (the runtime's file is gone), STALE (the copy in the configuration directory is gone or changed, or the definition, catalog, renderer or an extension changed since the render) or DRIFT (the file was edited by hand), each followed by the command that fixes it: qargi agent refresh NAME \-\-project P. Members are kept per project, so two projects can each have a worker.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
only the members of this project.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "rename " "OLD NEW"
Rename a member and its definition.
.br
Within its project. The project is \-\-project, else the one in effect, else the only project that has a member OLD; a name two or more projects hold, with none named or in effect, is refused (exit 2) and lists them.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project the member belongs to (default: the one in effect when it has the member, else the only project that has it).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "refresh " "NAME"
Re\-render a member from the member definition qargi ships now.
.br
The project is found as for rename. Keeps its name, roles and model, and writes the copy in the configuration directory and the runtime's file again: a file that is gone (a cleaned checkout, a fresh clone) comes back, a file edited by hand is replaced (the output says so). Use it after an upgrade changes the definition (for example its hook); an unchanged member is left alone. Sessions started afterwards use the new definition.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project the member belongs to (default: the one in effect when it has the member, else the only project that has it).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B models
Show the base members, the profiles with their resolved models and efforts, and each member.
.br
Two base members, one per guard class: worker (build, write, test; may write) and reviewer (review; read\-only). Everything else is a profile {base, tier, effort, purpose} resolved at dispatch: the shipped ones are in lib/model\-defaults.json (build, write, test, review, auditor, advisor, researcher, lead), a project adds or overrides its own under profiles.NAME in its answers, and a provider file (lib/providers/NAME.json; the project's models.provider) maps the tiers fast, balanced, deep and frontier to models. The frontier tier (researcher) is opt\-in. Problems are flagged on their row, never fatal: a member that is not a base (nothing dispatches it), a missing base, a refused project profile, a mixed\-class member, a tier with no alias, a frontier member outside researcher. A project is needed, because its models.provider decides the models: \-\-project, else the one in effect (with none, exit 2). The members shown are that project's (`agent list` shows all projects'). Read\-only.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
machine\-readable.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "remove " "NAME"
Delete a member's definition and its record.
.br
The project is found as for rename: \-\-project, else the one in effect, else the only project that has the member; a name in two or more projects with none named is refused (exit 2).
.RS
.TP
