.TP
.BI "check " "[PROJECT]"
Check a render for drift (read only).
.br
Needs \-\-target claude\-code. Writes nothing and exits 1 on any finding: library drift (a pack changed or failed review since it was rendered), rendered tampering (a file differs from the manifest, or an extra or missing file), enablement drift (a pack enabled or disabled, or its roles changed, since the render), shadowing of a rendered skill by ~/.claude/skills or by the checkout's .claude/skills, and a render directory that has no manifest (a broken render). A project with no render at all is not a failure: the check prints a not applied line with the command that applies it and exits 0. The answer packs.none: true says the project uses no project packs; the check then passes with no render, and fails if a pack is linked to a role of the project. It also reports a rendered file that differs from the library copy (even when the manifest was edited with it), a changed file mode, a group\- or world\-writable render, a malformed manifest, and a manifest that names another project or whose role scoping is not advisory.
It ends with a section, skills a session would load, that gives every skill a session opened in the checkout would load one state: qargi (a library pack with approved content, or a skill shipped with qargi), accepted (skill accept), drifted (accepted, but its content changed: this exits 1), excluded (switched off in the provider's settings; qargi only reads them) or unmanaged (everything else, shown with what to do about it; this does not change the exit status). When the checkout's skills directory is not linked to the render it adds a note naming packs link.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-target " "TARGET"
claude\-code renders for a local session; managed\-agents (the default for apply) is the cloud update.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "link " "[PROJECT]"
Make the project's checkout load its packs by itself.
.br
The provider's project skills directory in the repository (for Claude Code .claude/skills) becomes a symbolic link to the render made by packs apply \-\-target. Without \-\-approved it prints the plan, the skills a session would load, and a plan token; apply with \-\-plan TOKEN \-\-approved. If the directory is absent it creates the link; if it is already that link it says so and exits 0. It refuses a link to anywhere else, a symlinked parent, a checkout that is not the top of a git work tree, a missing render, and files tracked by git. If it is a real directory the plan lists each skill in it as same, differs or new against the library and offers skill import and skill link for the new ones; apply then moves the directory to the backup name (skills.qargi\-backup beside it) and links, so skills not imported stop loading until they are. Nothing is deleted. If git does not ignore the path it prints the line to add to .git/info/exclude and never edits that file. A provider that has no such directory is refused (exit 3). Every verb that takes \-\-target (packs apply, packs check), packs link and unlink, skill accept and marshal handoff exit 2 when the project has no provider (qargi project set P models.provider NAME). A local member's guard refuses it.
.RS
