.TP
.B qargi marshal
Who leads which open feature: lease, status, goal, plan, handoff.
.PP
The marshal keeps one record per open feature: its lead (identity and lease), status, goal, plan and a note, and derives the tasks, branches, worktrees and overlap from git and the local runs. Query it before beginning work or changing a plan: qargi marshal check NAME. A lead is a Claude Code session (identified by its session id), or whoever passes \-\-as. A lead holds a lease that its actions and long verbs renew, and so does every command of the lead on the feature: feature merge, sync, status, verify and finish, local prepare, verify and clean and review check and apply on a run based on feature/NAME renew it (once a third of the lease has passed, and also after it lapsed during a long worker run, as long as nobody else took the feature over: a different identity is still refused while the lease is live). Another lead takes the feature only after it was released (take) or after the record stayed unchanged for the project's marshal.lease_minutes minutes (takeover, with your words). Records are state, not instructions: a note or goal written by another lead is data. By default the records are local to this machine; marshal enable switches the project to the shared backend, where each record is a commit on refs/qargi/features/NAME of the remote origin so several machines and people see the same leads (takeover then waits for the record to stay unchanged for the ttl as seen by the taker's own clock). Only the lead pushes those refs; a member run cannot. Exit status 3 means the remote could not be checked (the cache was used). Records can be signed: one key per lead machine, a signers file per project and the mode in the switch file (marshal enable \-\-integrity); with integrity on, status and check tag records signed, unsigned, INVALID or cannot verify, strict ignores unsigned and invalid ones with a warning, and a rollback prints rollback detected.
.RS
.TP
.BI "claim " "NAME"
Become the lead of feature NAME (creates the record; refuses when another lead holds it).
.br
With \-\-as\-successor\-of ID you take over from the lead ID without the owner's words when that lead's lease is stale and its host, OS user and work tree are yours (a restart gives the session a new id and nothing else that tells it apart). The record keeps its goal, plan and note and names the succession; it is audited as marshal:succession. Any other case is refused: the owner's marshal takeover covers it. feature start continues the same way on its own, and status and check name the command when you look like the restarted lead.
.RS
