.TP
.B qargi release
Releases and hotfixes by the git\-flow rules.
.PP
release/<version> is cut from develop (a hotfix/<version> from master); its first commit bumps VERSION and dates the changelog and the man page. It is pushed and a pull request into master runs CI on the commit that will be released. Finishing merges it into master with \-\-no\-ff, tags it, merges it into develop (a hotfix: into the open release branch, if there is one) with \-\-no\-ff and deletes it. A release starts only from a develop whose head passed the whole suite. Starting, pushing, merging, tagging and publishing need the plan token and an approval; signing needs your terminal. The layout of what a release updates is the project's, set in its answers (all optional): release.version_file (the file that holds only the version; default VERSION; none when there is no such file, and the version is then the changelog heading and the tag), release.changelog.unreleased (the heading of the changes to come, default '## [Unreleased]'; it may carry the version, as in '## {version} (unreleased)', and is then renamed) and release.changelog.released (default '## [{version}] \- {date}'), release.version_files (other files that carry the version, a list of paths; default none: start, status and verify warn of the ones that do not contain the version, never bump them), release.man_pages (a list of paths; default the man/*.1 files), release.test_command (what release verify runs, a command line or a list of words; default test_command) and release.preflight (a command as a list of words, {version} replaced, run by start and push before they change anything; a non\-zero exit refuses and shows the tail of its output). The project answer gates (an ordered list of {name, command, tier}) replaces test_command and release.test_command when declared: release verify runs the feature\-tier gates, then the release\-tier gates. The release files are the project's too: release.assets_command (the command that builds them, a command line or a list of words with {version} replaced), which release assets runs in a clean worktree of the tag, and release.assets (files or globs to attach, {version} replaced), taken in that worktree by release publish. release.after_steps is a list of steps ({version} replaced), each a one\-line string (a command or a reminder: its text is its name) or an object {name, command, who (lead or owner), evidence (what shows it was done)}; the names are distinct. release finish prints them as 'after the release:' lines and records them as pending; release step marks one done; qargi never runs them. release.notes_file (a file relative to the repository, {version} replaced; default none) is the text release publish uses for the notes, and release push warns when it leaves out an item of the version's changelog section. A member may not set them: the lead runs them. A project with a documentation site on a detached branch sets site.enabled to true, and optionally site.branch (default gh\-pages when repo.detached lists it) and site.config (the site.json on that branch, default site/site.json, with the hand pages in the folder pages/ beside it): then release finish ends with 'next: qargi release site VERSION' (the methodology's after_release step) and release status has a site line.
.RS
.TP
.BI "start " "VERSION"
Cut release/VERSION from develop (hotfix/VERSION from master) and bump the version.
.br
Without \-\-approved it changes nothing: it prints the plan (the branch, the base it is cut from and that base's head, the one commit that bumps the version, the files the commit changes, the date) and a plan token. Apply it with the same command and \-\-plan TOKEN \-\-approved "<your go\-ahead>" (the token covers the head of the base and the date, so a plan that went stale is refused); the apply creates the branch and writes a line of the audit log. Nothing is pushed. The checks below run for the plan too, so a refusal comes before a token does. Before anything changes it checks what a release must update and refuses, naming the file and the fix: a man page (man/*.1) needs a .TH line with a date and a version field, which the first commit sets to the date and VERSION (the pages are release.man_pages, else man/*.1); a CHANGELOG.md needs content under its unreleased heading ('## [Unreleased]', or release.changelog.unreleased). The version file is release.version_file (default VERSION; none: no file is bumped). release.preflight runs before anything changes. A project without a man page or changelog is not checked for that part. A release (not a hotfix) ends by listing what it contains: the features merged into develop since the previous tag, the \-\-no\-ff merges whose message is "Merge branch 'feature/...'", one per line with the short sha. When master and the new branch share no history (no merge base: master is one snapshot commit, say) it says so and that release push joins them. With the answer release.version_files (a list of paths relative to the repository) it checks, once VERSION is bumped, that each listed file on the new branch contains the version (as a whole version: 0.2.1 is not found in 0.2.10) and prints a warning naming the ones that do not: bump these on the release branch (release fix makes the worktree). It is a warning, not a refusal, because the files are bumped on the branch start creates; without the answer nothing is checked.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-hotfix
a hotfix: branch from master, finish into master and the open release branch (or develop).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-date " "YYYY\-MM\-DD"
the changelog and man page date (default today).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-empty\-notes
release although the unreleased section of CHANGELOG.md ('## [Unreleased]' by default) is empty.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "status " "VERSION"
Show the branch, its commits and whether the whole suite passed at its head.
.br
The full\-suite verdict names the command that produced it, when it ran, how long it took and the mode the command names (\-j N, shard I/K), from the suite record (a record made before the duration was kept shows the command and the time only). When release.test_command is set and is not that command, a line of its own says so: the pass is then not the release gate (release verify runs release.test_command). With declared gates a 'gates:' line lists each with its result at the last run. It asks the live origin with git ls\-remote (QARGI_REMOTE_TIMEOUT seconds, default 10) where the branches are, not the last fetch, and says 'remote not checked' when origin cannot be reached. When the tag VERSION exists it also warns about a tag that has no GitHub release (asked with a read\-only gh release view, through QARGI_GH, default gh; a gh that is missing or not logged in is a note) and about a tagged commit that lacks .github/workflows/release.yml while the branch has it, because a pushed tag starts that workflow only when its own commit contains it. Warnings and notes never fail the verb. Only when the optional answer release.sign is set (the default is not) and the tag has no GitHub release, it also says 'awaiting signature' until qargi release sign recorded a signature that still holds (no signed file or signature changed), and then 'VERSION is ready for signing: qargi release sign VERSION' when the repository is at the tag with the files built, or what stands in the way. When site.enabled is true a site line says 'site: not built for VERSION', 'site: built at SHA, not pushed' or 'site: pushed' (the commit of qargi release site on the site branch, and whether the live origin has it). While the release branch exists and the repository has an origin it shows the checks of the branch's pull request (gh pr checks, through QARGI_GH): a verdict and one line per check with its state, pass, fail or pending; a missing gh, no pull request or no checks is a warning. With release.version_files set, a line says that every listed file contains VERSION on the branch, or warns of the ones that do not (bump these on the release branch). A branch that shares no history with master (no merge base) gets a warning that release push joins it. For a tag that exists it ends with a checklist of what is left after the release: whether origin has the tag, whether the GitHub release is published or missing, for each entry of the answer release.assets ({version} replaced; a glob by its base name) whether the release has it, for each file of release.sign.files whether its .sig is attached (UNSIGNED when not), and the project's own after\-steps: the ones release finish recorded, else the optional answer release.after_steps ({version} replaced). Each is shown as '[done] NAME (WHO, TIME, evidence: TEXT)' or '[pending] NAME (WHO): COMMAND', the done ones first and the pending ones last; a pending step of the owner reads 'waits for the owner: run COMMAND', and a step that needs evidence says so. qargi release step marks one done; the list is kept in a file under the state directory.
.RS
