.TP
.BI "finish " "VERSION"
After the checks pass: merge into master, tag, merge back, delete the branch, push.
.br
The plan lists the checks of the branch's pull request (gh pr checks: one line per check, pass, fail or pending) and refuses, without a token, while one fails or is pending, unless \-\-ignore\-checks WORDS is given; the plan token covers the words. A missing gh, no pull request or no checks is a warning in the plan, and the apply then refuses (the checks are read again) unless \-\-ignore\-checks is given. A clean worktree that release fix made is removed with the branch. Merging back may conflict, usually over the version number (the model says so). Nothing moves then: resolve in the worktree the message names, commit, and run the same command with \-\-resolved. It refuses a branch whose manual page has an empty .TH date or version, and, when release.version_file is none, one whose changelog has no section for VERSION. It also refuses, with the fix, a branch that shares no history with master (no merge base): run release push first, whose plan joins the history of master. It asks the live origin with git ls\-remote (QARGI_REMOTE_TIMEOUT seconds, default 10) where the branches are, not the last fetch, and says 'remote not checked' when origin cannot be reached. When the answer release.after_steps lists steps (a string, or an object {name, command, who, evidence}; {version} replaced), it prints each as an 'after the release: [pending] NAME (WHO): COMMAND' line, never runs them, and records them as pending under the state's release directory (PROJECT\-VERSION.after.json) for release status and release step. It ends with a 'next: ...' line for each after_release step of the methodology whose answer is true: with site.enabled, 'next: qargi release site VERSION'.
