Signing is optional and off by default: it applies only to a project whose answers hold release.sign (otherwise this exits 2 and nothing else changes). Run it in your terminal, in the project's repository checked out at the tag (git checkout \-\-detach VERSION, with the release files built from it), or after qargi release assets VERSION, which builds them in a worktree of the tag that sign then uses in place of the repository (the files are signed, verified and recorded there): after finish, before publish. For each file of the answer release.sign.files it runs ssh\-keygen \-Y sign \-f KEY \-n NAMESPACE FILE attached to your terminal (stdin, stdout and stderr are yours; nothing is captured and no input is logged), so you type the key's passphrase there and it never passes through qargi, a log or an agent; no ssh agent is asked. Then it runs release.sign.verify_command (a list of words, no shell) in the repository and records the result, the tag's commit and the hash of every signed file and signature under the state's release directory. Refused (exit 1) when stdin is no terminal, HEAD is not exactly the tag VERSION, on a branch other than master, or a tracked file is modified; refused (exit 2) in a member run, when QARGI_NESTED is set or when release.sign is not set. A member run cannot sign. The answers: release.sign.key (an ssh private key), release.sign.namespace, release.sign.files (relative to the repository) and optionally release.sign.verify_command.
