.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-evidence " "TEXT"
the words that show the item is done (they are archived with the item and the date).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B qargi ref
Record in the lead's ref journal a branch move or a branch that no flow verb made.
.PP
The ref journal ($QARGI_STATE/ref\-journal.jsonl) is the lead's record of the branch and tag moves qargi's verbs make (feature, release, branch prune, review apply, with their pushes); qargi local verify and local clean accept and name what it records. qargi ref record adds the one move a lead makes by hand that verify must still accept: the rewrite of an agent branch that keeps its content. qargi ref note adds the branches the lead made by hand or pushed (git branch, git push), so that verify does not blame the run for them.
.RS
.TP
.BI "record " "REF OLD NEW"
Rewrite an agent branch to a commit with the same tree, and journal the move with the reason.
.br
REF is an agent/* branch (agent/NAME or refs/heads/agent/NAME). It refuses NEW unless git rev\-parse OLD^{tree} and NEW^{tree} are the same, and REF unless it is at OLD now; the move is then a compare\-and\-swap (git update\-ref REF NEW OLD), so nothing moves when REF changed meanwhile. The journal line has verb ref:rewrite and the reason, and a run prepared before the move verifies and is cleaned with it named as a rewrite; local clean, local list, lead compact\-check and feature finish follow the applied tip of a run to NEW (one test, lib/merged.py). No working tree, other ref or origin is touched. Not available in a member run.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-rewrite
the kind of move: REF goes from OLD to NEW, whose trees must be identical.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-why " "TEXT"
the reason, journaled and named by local verify.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "note " "REF"
Journal a branch you made by hand, or pushed to origin, so that local verify accepts it.
.br
REF (give several) is a branch NAME, or origin/NAME for the branch NAME of origin, whose live value is asked (git ls\-remote); the branch must exist now. The journal line (verb ref:note) goes from the ref being absent to the value it has now, or, when you noted that ref before, from where the earlier note left it (pushing the same branch again); a second note is refused when the earlier note's value is not an ancestor of the current one (the ref was rewritten: inspect it; qargi ref record \-\-rewrite records a rewrite of an agent branch that keeps its tree). The note prints the author and subject of the tip, and REF@SHA (7 to 40 hex digits) notes REF only while it is at SHA, so the lead notes what it looked at. Nothing is created, moved or pushed. A run prepared before it then verifies with the branch named as one you noted. Only a ref that did not exist when the run was prepared (or that you noted before) is accepted this way: a ref that existed then and has moved, a deletion, and any ref you did not note stay offending, so a branch the run's agent made or moved is still found. Note only what you made. Not available in a member run. The harness's own worktree branches (worktree\-bridge\-NAME, held by the locked worktree bridge\-NAME of the harness) need no note: verify leaves them out.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-why " "TEXT"
a reason, journaled with a local branch.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B qargi start
Start a standby cloud session for an instance.
.PP
Cloud only, and only when your request names it. The session's spend ceiling comes from the deployment.
.RS
.nf
qargi start NAME
.fi
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-label " "SLUG"
a task slug for the session title.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-role " "ROLE"
the role a base session runs (required for <project>\-worker and <project>\-reviewer).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-profile " "PROFILE"
the profile of the role (default: the role's own).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-opt\-in
confirm an opt\-in profile such as researcher.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-asked " "REQUEST"
the request, in your words, that names the cloud instance; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.TP
.B qargi send
Send a task to an idle cloud session.
.PP
The text is the whole brief: TASK, DONE (a command whose exit status decides it), BASE, SCOPE and so on. The session is titled <instance>\-<label>; use the same slug for the agent/<slug> branch.
.RS
.nf
qargi send NAME TASK
.fi
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-label " "SLUG"
the task slug (required).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-session " "SID"
send to this session.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-new
start a new session first.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-role " "ROLE"
the role a base session runs (required for <project>\-worker and <project>\-reviewer).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-profile " "PROFILE"
the profile of the role (default: the role's own).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-opt\-in
confirm an opt\-in profile such as researcher.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-asked " "REQUEST"
the request, in your words, that names the cloud instance; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.TP
.B qargi status
Show tracked cloud sessions, or one session.
.RS
.nf
qargi status [SID|NAME]
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B qargi report
Cost per applied change, what waits for review, what looks stuck.
.PP
Read\-only. From the session tracker: per instance and in total, the sessions by review state and status, the spend (list_cost, in the units of the budget flags: cents, per the docs) and the spend per applied session, how long each session pending review has waited (oldest first), the sessions that look stale (running, or idle and pending review, with no refresh for \-\-stale\-after), the idle sessions split by why they stopped when that is recorded, and the time from creation to close. A second section lists local runs from the run directories qargi local prepare made: finished (manifest DONE or BLOCKED), no manifest and still changing, or no manifest and quiet for \-\-local\-stale\-after (possibly stalled). The output says what it cannot tell you; it never prints a session's note or request.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-since " "DATE"
only sessions and runs started on or after this date (2026\-10\-01).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-instance " "NAME"
only this instance's sessions (leaves the local runs out).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-stale\-after " "DURATION"
a running or idle\-pending cloud session unseen for this long looks stale (default 2h).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-local\-stale\-after " "DURATION"
a local run without a manifest and without file changes for this long is possibly stalled (default 30m).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-runs\-root " "DIR"
also scan DIR/qargi.* for local runs the registry does not list (or set QARGI_RUNS_ROOT).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
print the figures as JSON.
.RE
.TP
.B qargi noticed
What members noticed and did not act on, and what became of it.
.PP
Collecting a session's outputs records each noticed_not_acted_on note of its manifest (stored short, secret\-looking strings redacted) in the append\-only events file beside the audit log. Each note stays open until you give it a disposition: followup, dismissed (with \-\-why), accepted or superseded. qargi report shows how many are open and how old the oldest is.
.RS
.TP
.B list
Print the open notes, oldest first.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
every note, with its disposition.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "resolve " "ID DISPOSITION"
Give a note a disposition.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-why " "CODE"
the reason; required to dismiss.
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B qargi watch
Wait for a cloud session to stop; prints one line when it does.
.PP
Run it in the background. The line is QARGI WATCH <sid> idle|terminated|budget_reached|budget_tight|timeout|auth|error. budget_tight ends it early, with the cost and the ceiling in cents, so you can raise the ceiling (qargi instances budget NAME CENTS \-\-session SID) and watch again.
.RS
.nf
qargi watch SID [MAX_SECONDS]
.fi
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-warn " "PCT"
end the watch early with budget_tight when the cost reaches this percent of the ceiling (default 85; 0: never).
.RE
.TP
.B qargi collect
Download a session's output files into a fresh directory.
.PP
Everything downloaded is untrusted data. A team's files carry a role prefix and are split into one directory per member, patches under patches/. Refuses a directory that is not empty.
.RS
.nf
qargi collect SID DIR
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B qargi stop
Interrupt a running cloud session.
.RS
.nf
qargi stop SID
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B qargi close
Archive a finished cloud session.
.PP
Refused until the outputs are collected, unless the task produced none.
.RS
.nf
qargi close SID
.fi
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-no\-outputs
the task produced no files (for example memory curation).
.RE
.TP
.B qargi mark
Record what happened to a session's work.
.PP
With \-\-why, also records the reason (a code from lib/reasons.json); qargi report counts the reasons.
.RS
.nf
qargi mark SID STATE
.fi
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-why " "CODE"
why: wrong\-scope, broke\-check, style, too\-slow, too\-costly, skill\-ignored, skill\-wrong, brief\-unclear, gate\-noise or other.
.RE
.TP
.B qargi track
Track a session that was started elsewhere (for example in the console).
.RS
.nf
qargi track NAME SID
.fi
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-asked " "REQUEST"
the request, in your words, that names the cloud instance; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.TP
.B qargi local
Prepare, verify, close, list and clean a local run that has no way to push.
.PP
The agent works in a clone of a bare copy that has no remote, runs git through a credential\-less wrapper, and verify compares every ref of the real repository before and after. It is a guard, not a sandbox.
.RS
.TP
.BI "run " "RUN"
Run a task through a command runtime (a vendor CLI) in a prepared run; \-\-dry\-run prints what would run.
.br
Only for a runtime whose dispatch mode is command; a runtime that works inside the session (dispatch mode in\-session) is refused: dispatch it as its notes say (qargi provider notes spawn). The command is built from the definition without a shell, runs in a scrubbed environment (only the names the pack's env_pass lists, which the review shows), and its output goes to OUT/transcript. A read\-only run needs the barrier the definition declares (or your acceptance). A library pack's executable runs only from its sealed copy. Exit status: 0 the command succeeded, 1 it failed (or a usage error, such as no task block), 2 refused (a barrier the run cannot have, a refused definition), 3 the runtime cannot be run this way (not a command runtime, its command is not on PATH), 4 transport (the definition maps the command's exit to it).
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-task " "FILE"
the file with the task block (default RUN/task.md).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-model " "MODEL"
a tier or a model id the runtime accepts (default the balanced tier).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-effort " "LEVEL"
an effort level; dropped with a note when the runtime cannot map it.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-timeout " "S"
seconds before the process group is killed (default from the definition).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-again
run a second time in the same run.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
print the argv, the working directory and the prompt, and run nothing.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B prepare
Make the clone and the wrapper for a local agent.
.br
Prints KEY=value lines: RUN, ROLE, CLASS, PROFILE (the role's own unless \-\-profile), MEMBER, MODEL and EFFORT (only with \-\-profile), CLONE, OUT, TMP, GIT, BASE_SHA, CONTRACT (the run's own copy of the member contract, with the contract addenda of the project's local runtime as runtime\-addenda.md) and, when the role declares submodules (roles.ROLE.tools, kind submodule) and the clone got them, SUBMODULES=path,... Each declared submodule is checked out in the clone from the lead's copy of its objects (the checkout at REPO/PATH, else the modules directory), offline: a mirror with no remote under RUN/sub and git submodule update from it, push URL disabled. A commit the lead's copy lacks, a submodule not initialised there, or a run made with \-\-repo\-url, is a note on stderr; nothing is fetched. Then a blank line and a TASK\-BLOCK PATHS block (RUN_DIR, CLONE, OUT, TMP, GIT, BASE_SHA, CONTRACT) to copy into the task, and in a stored project a TASK\-BLOCK MEMORY block. A caller that evals the output must stop at the blank line.
When the project (found by matching the repository against the stored answers) has a render for its local runtime (packs apply \-\-target RUNTIME) that has drifted, prepare also prints a WARNING block to stderr with the findings and the fix, packs apply \-\-target RUNTIME PROJECT. The run goes on, and stdout and the TASK\-BLOCK PATHS block are unchanged.
A \-\-scratch on a tmpfs (/dev/shm, often /tmp) is warned about on stderr: a reboot empties it and loses the run (its clone, commits and output); prefer a directory on disk, such as ~/.cache/qargi\-runs.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-repo " "DIR"
the local repository.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-repo\-url " "URL"
clone a private repository from this https URL (no user name or password in it: that is \-\-credential\-file).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-credential\-file " "FILE"
token file for \-\-repo\-url.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-base " "BRANCH"
the base branch.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-slug " "SLUG"
the task slug (branch agent/<slug>).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-scratch " "DIR"
where to put the run.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-role " "ROLE"
the task's role, written to RUN/ability, with its class in RUN/class and in your registry (the registry's decides); a read\-only role keeps the clone unchanged (default build).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-profile " "NAME"
the profile to dispatch under (for example advisor or auditor for review); resolved before any clone, refused when its class does not fit the role, recorded in RUN/profile and printed with MEMBER, MODEL and EFFORT.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "verify " "[RUN_DIR]"
Compare the repository's refs with the snapshot taken by prepare.
.br
OUT without a report, for an uncollected run or a read\-only run, is said with its likely cause: a runtime can refuse the member's write tool for a report file, so the file never reached OUT; the member is told to write its OUT files with the shell (a heredoc) and to collect again (for a read\-only run it is a note, and the exit status stays the refs verdict). Fails first, with exit 1 and "OUT is empty: the member did not collect", when the clone holds commits past BASE_SHA and RUN/out holds neither patches nor a report: the refs are compared all the same and their verdict is printed next to that message (exit 2 when they changed), but nothing is recorded: the run is not verified, and the member must collect (its DONE needs ls OUT/patches to list the patches) before apply or close. Exits 2 when refs changed (1 before), 3 when the lead's registry does not vouch for the run: it is not in it, names no repository for it, or RUN/repo.path, repo.url or cred.path differ from it (the repository and the credential come from the registry; a member can write the run directory). It accepts the lead's own moves and names each one ("develop moved by qargi feature finish"): what the lead's ref journal records (the branches and tags that qargi's flow verbs move, push to origin or delete: feature finish and park, release and hotfix finish and push, branch prune, review apply, the parking branch following develop, a rewrite recorded with qargi ref record \-\-rewrite), a remote\-tracking ref of origin that says what origin has (a fetch), the parking branch (repo.parking_branch, default scratch) fast\-forwarded to develop's tip, by hand too, and the branches and branches of origin you noted (qargi ref note: a ref that did not exist when the run was prepared; one the run's agent could have made or moved and you did not note still fails). It also leaves out the harness's own bridge branches: a branch worktree\-bridge\-NAME while the locked worktree bridge\-NAME of the harness (in its worktrees directory under the main checkout) holds it and its moves are fast\-forwards (or once the worktree and the branch are gone). The refs a runtime or harness creates in the repository can be declared in the project answer runs.owned_refs, a list of glob patterns for full ref names (qargi project set PROJECT runs.owned_refs '["refs/heads/foo\-*"]'; only the lead sets it, and project validate checks it): a local ref that matches one is accepted in either phase and named ("foo\-x created; accepted: refs/heads/foo\-* (owned by the runtime, per runs.owned_refs)"). A pattern needs a literal prefix of at least 3 characters after refs/NAMESPACE/, may not match develop, master, a repo.long_lived branch, or be able to match anything inside the git\-flow branches (agent/, feature/, release/, hotfix/) or the tags (rel*/0* is refused as release/ is), and a ref is never taken as owned when it is the base of a run that is not cleaned or closed, a worktree\-bridge\-* branch, a git\-flow branch or a tag; a ref that matches no valid pattern is checked as before, in the strict phase too. The answers verify and clean read (runs.owned_refs, repo.long_lived, repo.parking_branch) are the copy prepare took into your registry, not the stored file of the day: a change of them takes effect on the runs prepared after it. After a passing verify the run records RUN/verified and its line in the registry is marked verified (what marshal status and handoff list the task as, also after clean); later verify and clean compare with that and also accept your fast\-forwards of develop and master and of agent|feature|release|hotfix/* branches, and new branches there. Anything else (a push, a tag, a deletion or a rewrite nobody journaled) fails. Run it on every run before merging any of them. When it passes it copies OUT, a run bundle beside it and the snapshots to <state>/runs/<run id>/, so a run lost with its directory (a reboot that emptied a tmpfs) can still be applied: qargi review apply \-\-from\-saved ID. \-\-saved ID compares the refs against that copy's snapshots.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-saved " "ID"
verify the lead's saved copy of a passed run whose directory is gone (ID: the name of the run directory, qargi.XXXXXX).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "close " "RUN_DIR"
Close a verified run whose patches you applied by hand (git am): it is no longer open.
.br
Marks the run in the lead's registry (closed: the time, closed_by: applied\-by\-am), never in the run directory, which a member can write; the files are kept. A closed run is no longer listed as open or counted as applied and not merged by qargi lead compact\-check and the compaction advice, no longer blocks qargi feature finish and is listed by marshal status as closed; qargi local list shows closed in the APPLIED column. qargi local clean then removes it without asking for an applied tip (its commits are taken as applied by hand); clean \-\-applied removes it too. Refused for a run that has no passing local verify (verify it first: close must not hide changed refs), a run whose refs changed since that verify (close runs the refs check again), a read\-only run, one applied with qargi review apply (merge that branch), a run already closed or cleaned. When the applied branch was rewritten by hand (its tip is not the review:apply tip in your ref journal, the trees are the same and no rewrite is recorded), the refusal for an applied run also names the fix: qargi ref record \-\-rewrite BRANCH OLD NEW \-\-why ..., with the branch and both tips filled in. Not available in a member run.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-applied\-by\-am
the way the run was taken in: you applied its patches by hand with git am (the only way close knows; required).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "clean " "[RUN_DIR]"
Remove a collected, verified run and its temporary directory.
.br
Prepare makes a private temporary directory for the run (TMP, on /dev/shm when it exists) that the agent puts its test sandboxes in; clean removes it with the clone, the bare copy and the output (only when it is a qargi\-run.XXXXXX directory under /dev/shm or QARGI_RUN_TMP, or inside the run). It refuses a run whose output has no manifest (collect first), except a read\-only run (an advisor or a review: its product is a report, never patches) whose output holds a report.md (or NAME\-report.md; a regular file, directly in OUT), which closes with a plain clean when its refs are unchanged and its clone holds no commit; and one whose repository refs changed (that is evidence); the lead's own moves that verify accepts (a feature finish since the run was prepared, for one) are no change and are named in its output. It also refuses a run whose clone holds commits that were never applied (review apply) or whose applied tip is on no branch but agent/* in the repository (feature merge first), so finished work is not deleted by mistake. \-\-discard is the deliberate way past all of these; it still refuses anything that prepare did not make. A run cleaned with \-\-applied needs a RUN/applied record and a repository path in the registry (a run of a repository URL is kept); \-\-applied takes no RUN_DIR and no \-\-discard. A cleaned run keeps its line in the registry, marked cleaned: marshal status and handoff list it as cleaned, never as an open task. \-\-stale DAYS \-\-scratch DIR is a plan first: it prints each finished run older than DAYS days and changes nothing; with \-\-yes each is cleaned exactly as clean RUN would, so a stale run whose commits were never applied or merged is kept and named (exit status 1), never dropped. It is refused without \-\-scratch, takes no RUN_DIR, no \-\-applied and no \-\-discard, and \-\-yes is for \-\-stale only. A run whose directory is gone (a reboot, or removed by hand) is the registry's record and the saved copy alone: clean RUN drops both once the saved work is applied and merged, and refuses otherwise; clean RUN \-\-discard drops them whatever they hold. \-\-stale counts such a run by the start time of its record, and with \-\-yes drops it unless its saved copy holds work that was never applied (kept and named). When the applied branch is not merged because you rewrote it by hand (its tip is not the review:apply tip in your ref journal, the trees are the same and no rewrite is recorded), the refusal also names the fix, with the branch and both tips filled in: git branch \-f BRANCH OLD, then qargi ref record \-\-rewrite BRANCH OLD NEW \-\-why ...; clean then follows the rewrite. When the refusal for an OUT with no manifest finds no report either, it names the likely cause too: a runtime can refuse the member's write tool for a report file, so the files never reached OUT; the member writes them with the shell (a heredoc). \-\-orphans is for what no run owns: prepare makes a qargi\-run.XXXXXX directory under /dev/shm (QARGI_RUN_TMP moves it) for each run, and clean RUN removes only the one your registry names for that run, so a prepare that failed halfway, a dropped record or a replaced state directory leaves one behind. \-\-orphans lists every such directory with its age (from its modification time) and says whether a run of the registry names it, live or not; a link and a file are named and left. Only the registry of this state directory is consulted: the runs of another state directory on the host (another QARGI_STATE or QARGI_HOME) are not, so their directories look orphaned once they are older than \-\-days; check the age and the directory before you approve. Without \-\-approved it prints the plan and its token and removes nothing. With \-\-plan TOKEN \-\-approved WORDS it removes only the directories that no registry names, that are older than \-\-days (default 2, at least 1) and have nothing in them newer; each is checked again at the moment of removal, a directory that appeared or changed since the plan makes the token stale, and the removal is in the audit log with your words. A named directory is never removed by it, whatever its age. It takes no RUN_DIR, \-\-applied, \-\-discard, \-\-stale, \-\-yes or \-\-scratch; \-\-days, \-\-plan and \-\-approved are for it only. A clean marks the run's record cleaned and removes its saved copy.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-discard
drop the run on purpose: also a run whose output holds no manifest, whose commits were never merged or whose repository refs changed (verify's outcome is printed as a warning).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-applied
clean every run under \-\-scratch whose work is recorded applied (review apply) and merged into a branch other than agent/*; the others are kept and named, and the exit status is 1 when an applied run was kept.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-stale " "DAYS"
list the finished runs (RUN/verified or RUN/applied exists) under \-\-scratch that are at least DAYS days old, from the run's start (a run with no start time is named as skipped); with \-\-yes clean each of them by the rules below, and keep and name those clean refuses; a run still in progress is never listed.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-yes
with \-\-stale: clean the runs it lists (without it nothing changes).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-orphans
list the qargi\-run.XXXXXX directories under /dev/shm (or QARGI_RUN_TMP) that no run of your registry names, each with its age and whether it is named; a plan and a token, nothing removed.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-days " "DAYS"
with \-\-orphans: remove only the orphans older than DAYS days, and with nothing in them newer (default 2, at least 1).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
with \-\-orphans: the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
with \-\-orphans and \-\-plan: your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-scratch " "DIR"
with \-\-applied or \-\-stale: the directory the runs were prepared in; only the run directories directly under it are looked at.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B list
List the local runs: role, slug, age, whether applied and merged.
.br
Read\-only. One row per run: ROLE, SLUG, AGE, APPLIED (RUN/applied exists: review apply ran; closed for a run closed with close \-\-applied\-by\-am), MERGED (the applied tip is on a branch other than agent/*; ? for a run of a repository URL) and RUN. It is the list that qargi local clean \-\-applied \-\-scratch DIR acts on. A directory is a run only when the lead's registry names it (a directory a member makes beside its own run is not one), and the AGE is ? when the run's started file is not a number. A run of the registry whose directory is gone and that clean did not remove is listed after the others as directory gone, with its saved copy and the review apply \-\-from\-saved command that applies it.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-scratch " "DIR"
the directory the runs were prepared in (default: the runs of the registry that still exist).
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B qargi supervise
Find stalled agent runs: notify, prepare a nudge, never act alone.
.PP
Hooks only record (the run\-event verbs of qargi hook append ids, times, a tool name and a hash to a per\-run file under the state directory; no command, message or error text). One idempotent tick is the only actor: it folds those files, the local\-run registry and the session tracker into runs.json and classifies each unfinished run in a fixed order: waiting on an approval (not a stall), stopped by an API error, interim (turn ended, background work running, a cap of about 35 minutes), looping (three identical tool digests in a row), suspected dead (no activity for too long); for cloud sessions requires_action, budget_reached, stale while running, idle awaiting review. It acts only by printing `QARGI SUPERVISE <class> <run> <next\-action>` lines, appending to supervisor.log and the audit log (origin supervisor), and PREPARING a nudge in nudges.jsonl. A nudge is a fixed template, at most 2 per run with at least 10 minutes between them, never while the run waits on an approval or has little budget left, and never an approval. A script cannot message a local subagent: the coordinating session delivers the prepared nudge and records it with delivered, and only then does it count. A cloud nudge is sent only by nudge, which needs \-\-asked. Nothing sends automatically; the thresholds are data in lib/supervise\-defaults.json, conservative floors until `qargi report` shows real run lengths. Run the tick on a timer or a loop; status warns when it has not run for twice its interval.
.RS
.TP
.B tick
Fold the events, classify the runs, print what needs attention, prepare nudges.
.br
Run twice with nothing new it prints nothing the second time.
.RS
