.SS Lead and credentials
.TP
.B qargi lead
The lead session: its context, and the cloud lead's right to push.
.PP
usage and compact\-check keep the lead's own context small: usage says how much of it is in use, compact\-check says whether a /compact is safe now and what to re\-read after it. Only the lead ever pushes, never a member. With a grant it creates agent/* branches and opens pull requests; every merge is asked of you, and it cannot publish releases. grant and revoke are interactive: each step prints its plan and asks you at the terminal, and your answer is the recorded approval. From Claude Code run them with !.
.RS
.TP
.B usage
How much of this session's context is in use: tokens of the last turn, percent of the window, transcript size.
.br
The runtime of the project says where its session transcripts are and how to read them (resources.transcripts of its definition): the context in use is the input, cache\-read and cache\-creation tokens of the last assistant turn. The window is the project answer lead.context_window (tokens), else the one the runtime's definition gives for the model id of that turn (resources.transcripts.windows; the entries of the shipped definition match ids that end in [1m] or \-1m, so an id without such a suffix matches none), else the definition's single window, which is only a guess: the lines that show a percentage then say (window assumed N; set lead.context_window), and \-\-json says window_source answer, model or assumed. When the tokens in use exceed that guessed window the real window is larger: the text then says tokens exceed the assumed window N instead of a percentage, and \-\-json keeps percent and sets window_exceeded to true. Read only; a transcript that cannot be found or read prints unknown and exits 0.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-session " "ID"
read the transcript of this session id (default: QARGI_LEAD, else the newest of this directory).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-transcript " "FILE"
read this transcript file.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
machine\-readable.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B compact\-check
Is a /compact safe now? Exit 0 safe, 1 not, with every reason and the block to re\-read after it.
.br
Not safe while a local run is prepared and not verified, verified and not applied, or applied and not merged; a merge waits for its resolution; a whole\-suite run (feature verify, release verify) is running; the handoff note written by marshal handoff \-\-write is older than the last merge; or a release or hotfix branch is open. An applied run counts as merged by the test local list and local clean use: its applied tip is on a branch other than agent/*, or the commit the tip was rewritten to (ref record \-\-rewrite) is, wherever the run directory lies. It prints the restart block: the handoff note, qargi marshal status, qargi feature status and the open runs, so the lead resumes from stored state. Read only. qargi cannot run /compact itself: the lead (or the person) does. feature finish, the last feature merge of a feature's runs, release finish and marshal handoff print one line when the context is at or above the project answer lead.compact_advice_pct (default 40; 0 turns it off).
.RS
