.SS Export and import
.TP
.B qargi export
Save a project's setup, definitions and memories to one file.
.PP
Writes one archive of the parts you select: config (project answers, instances, profiles, the skill and tool library), definitions (the cloud agents, deployments and environment as they are now, secrets removed), memories (every note of the memory and reference stores), state (session tracker, audit log) and credentials (token files; only in an encrypted archive). It asks what to include and for an optional password; with one, the whole archive is encrypted by gpg (AES\-256) and the password never goes into a command line. It only reads: nothing is changed or deleted.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "NAMES"
only these projects, comma separated (default: ask, or all).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-only " "PARTS"
the parts: config, definitions, memories, state, credentials (default: ask).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-out " "FILE"
the file to write (default: qargi\-export\-<time>.tar.gz[.gpg] here).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-password\-file " "FILE"
encrypt with the first line of this file.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-no\-encrypt
write the archive without a password (for scripts).
.RE
.TP
.B qargi import
Restore from an export, choosing what and how.
.PP
Reads an archive made by qargi export (asking for the password when it is encrypted), shows what is inside, asks which parts and projects to import and, for each part, how to treat what already exists: merge (add what is missing), overwrite (also replace what differs) or replace (make it match the archive, removing the rest). It then prints the plan and writes nothing until you confirm; replaced and removed items are saved under the state directory first. The archive is treated as untrusted data: names and checksums are verified. Definitions are restored as files only (rebuild with provision). Memory notes go to the stores named in the project's answers. An archive's project answers never add or change models.extensions or models.accept_no_barrier (they take a plan token and an approval: qargi provider enable, disable, accept): the stored value stays, or the key is left out, and a note says so. It asks at a terminal; \-\-dry\-run shows the plan without one.
.RS
.nf
qargi import FILE
.fi
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-only " "PARTS"
the parts to import (default: ask).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "NAMES"
only these projects of the archive, comma separated.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-mode " "MODE"
merge, overwrite or replace for every part (default: ask for each).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-password\-file " "FILE"
the password is the first line of this file.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
print the plan and stop; changes nothing.
.RE
.SS Other
.TP
.B qargi upgrade
Move the installed tool to a newer branch or tag of its origin.
.PP
Works on the root\-owned checkout install.sh recorded (for example /opt/qargi). Without \-\-approved it fetches the target, shows the commits and files that would change and prints a plan token. With \-\-plan TOKEN \-\-approved "..." it applies exactly that: a branch on the current branch moves forward only (fast\-forward), another branch is checked out at its tip, a tag is checked out detached; install.sh \-\-user then runs as you and the members to refresh are listed. The git commands that write into the checkout run through sudo (QARGI_SUDO names another command). When the members file (~/.qargi/config/agents.json) is not in the current shape, which every agent verb refuses, the plan and the end of the upgrade say so and how to redo it; nothing is changed for that. A member or agent cannot use it.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-branch " "BRANCH"
the branch to move to (default: the installed checkout's current branch).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-tag " "TAG"
a tag to check out instead.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-plan " "TOKEN"
the plan token printed by the same command without \-\-approved.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-approved " "WORDS"
your go\-ahead for exactly that plan, in your words; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.TP
.B qargi help
Show help.
.PP
qargi help shows the commands; qargi help COMMAND the verbs and options of one; qargi help TOPIC explains an idea. Every command also takes \-\-help.
.RS
.nf
qargi help [COMMAND|TOPIC]
.fi
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-man
print the manual page source (roff); view it with: qargi help \-\-man | man \-l \-.
.RE
.TP
.B qargi version
Print the version.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Once per project:
.nf
    qargi project template acme > acme.json   # edit it
    qargi project init acme \-\-answers acme.json
    qargi provision plan acme\-build    # then apply it with \-\-plan and \-\-approved
.fi
.PP
One task, cloud:
.nf
    qargi send acme\-build "$BRIEF" \-\-label rename\-verb \-\-asked "use the cloud builder"
    qargi watch SID    # in the background
    qargi collect SID ./out
    qargi review check ./out
.fi
.PP
A team:
.nf
    qargi team brief acme code\-docs \-\-task "rename the verb" \-\-done "make test" \-\-base develop
    qargi lead status acme
    qargi team check ./run
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.qargi/config/projects/NAME.json
the answers about a project.
.TP
.I ~/.qargi/config/instances.json
the registry of instances.
.TP
.I ~/.qargi/config/profiles/NAME.md
a repository's gates and review rules, given to agents with a task.
.TP
.I ~/.qargi/config/packs/
the library of skills and tools, and their links.
.TP
.I ~/.qargi/config/credentials/
token files, mode 600.
.TP
.I ~/.qargi/config/install.json
the tool root install.sh recorded; a checkout run from elsewhere is a dev run.
.TP
.I ~/.qargi/state/sessions.json
the tracker of cloud sessions.
.TP
.I ~/.qargi/state/audit.log
append\-only record of every approved or cloud verb, with your words.
.TP
.I ~/.qargi/state/runs/
collected outputs of runs.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QARGI_HOME
the directory that holds config/ and state/ (default ~/.qargi; a dev run: <checkout>/.qargi, see qargi help dev).
.TP
.B QARGI_CONF
the configuration directory (default $QARGI_HOME/config); wins over QARGI_HOME.
.TP
.B QARGI_STATE
the state directory (default $QARGI_HOME/state); wins over QARGI_HOME.
.TP
.B QARGI_RUNS_ROOT
a directory to scan for local run directories (qargi.*) in qargi report, besides the registry.
.TP
.B QARGI_PROJECT
the project to use when the current directory is not inside its repository.
.TP
.B QARGI_ROLES
the list of work roles (default: build review write test).
.TP
.B QARGI_DRY_RUN
print a session request, with secrets masked, instead of sending it; qargi env apply stops before it runs the bootstrap.
.TP
.B QARGI_ENV_TIMEOUT
seconds the dry run of qargi env plan and apply may take (default 120).
.TP
.B QARGI_HOOK_MARSHAL_SECS
seconds the session\-start hook gives the read of the open features (qargi marshal status \-\-all \-\-brief \-\-no\-fetch) before it leaves the block out (default 5; a whole number from 1 to 9999, anything else is ignored); a test on a loaded machine raises it.
.TP
.B QARGI_BUSCTL
the program qargi doctor asks logind with (default busctl).
.TP
.B QARGI_DOCTOR_MIN_FREE
the percent of free space under /dev/shm below which qargi doctor warns (default 10).
.TP
.B QARGI_LOGINCTL
the program qargi doctor reads the user's linger with (default loginctl).
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
turn off bold in the terminal help.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. 1 when a check fails or a command is refused (the message says why). 2 for a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
The skills qargi\-flow, qargi\-instances, qargi\-memory, qargi\-packs, qargi\-project, qargi\-providers, qargi\-provision, qargi\-session, qargi\-task, qargi\-team and qargi\-verify, loaded by the lead's session (qargi help roles for the roles, which come as role text with the task block).
